  "authorshipStatus": "traditional",
  "author": "patanjali",
  "attributionNote": "The sutras are ascribed to Patanjali and the commentary that accompanies them to Vyasa, an author otherwise unknown who cannot be the compiler of the Mahabharata. The two were already transmitted as one text, the Patanjalayogasastra, and the edition followed here is the one Agashe printed at Poona in 1904.",
  "description": "The Yoga Sutras define yoga as the stilling of the movements of the mind and then set out what stands in the way, what practice does, what the mind becomes in concentration, and the discipline of dispassion that ends in the isolation of the seer. The Bhashya explains each aphorism in prose, supplying the argument and the illustrations the sutras leave out, and it is the reason the text became a system rather than a collection of maxims.",
  "historicalContext": "The combined text is the founding work of the Yoga school, and its reading of liberation as the isolation of pure awareness is the classical alternative to the Vedanta account. Its descriptions of practice were taken up by later compendia such as the Hatha Yoga Pradipika, which the library holds in translation.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete: all four padas and 195 units of the GRETIL e-text, streamed at read time one pada at a time. The sutras are printed in bold within the commentary in the source file and the two cannot be separated without losing the sense, so they are read as one continuous text; the commentary is not carried in a pane of its own.",
